+				look: ['{n} staff at the client want {font} installed. Checked with Helen how we normally put this.\n\n{PASTE:account}', 'Client wants {font} on {n} machines. Each person would be using the font in their own documents. Standard answer, but asked Helen to confirm the wording.'],
+				helen: ['Same answer as always: a font installed on someone’s machine is that person using the library. Suggest document templates with the fixed parts as artwork and an everyday font for the text they type. Any of them can of course take out their own subscription.'],
+				ends: [
+					[['a', 'Thanks, that helps. Installing {font} on {n} computers would mean {n} people using it, and each Pro subscription is for one person, so it isn’t something your subscription can cover. A few options that have worked for other designers:\n\n- Build their templates so the headings and logo are fixed artwork (outlined text or images) and the parts they type are set in a font already on their computers, such as Arial or Georgia. Your guidelines can name that as the brand’s everyday font.\n- Keep making the pieces that need {font} yourself, as part of your work for them.\n- Anyone at {client} who wants {font} on their own machine can take out their own subscription.\n\nI know that’s not the yes you were hoping for.'], ['c', 'That makes sense. I’ll go with the template idea and name Georgia as the everyday font in the guidelines. Thanks for the clear answer.']],
+					[['a', 'Thanks for finding out. In that case it isn’t covered: a Pro subscription is for one person, and {n} people setting their own documents in {font} would be {n} people using the library. What usually works is a set of templates where the logo and headings are fixed artwork and everything they type is in a font they already have. Or anyone there can take out their own subscription.'], ['c', 'Bit of a pain, but fair. Can you check I’ve understood: if I send them a Word template where the heading is an image and the rest is in Calibri, that’s fine?'], ['a', 'Yes, exactly that. The heading is artwork you made, and Calibri is theirs already.']]
+				],
+				settled: true
+			},
